truncated = _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n        if truncated != message:\n            record = logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)\n            record.msg = truncated\n            record.args = None  # getMessage returns str(msg) if no args\n            record.message = truncated\n            record.exc_text = None  # ensure recomputation? \n```\n\nHmm, exc_info kept in copy dict, fine. But `record.message` attribute already set? Actually record.message only set when Formatter.format runs. If format called twice on same record (two handlers), first handler sets record.message on the original record (logging.Formatter.format sets `record.message = record.getMessage()`), which is fine; after our copy, we set message explicitly.\n\nFor multi-line handling, the existing code uses record.message. In format, `msg = super().format(record)` uses record.message after substitution internally. Then if \"\\n\" in msg and record.message: prefix computed by find(record.message). If we copied record and set record.message = truncated, that works.\n\nBut careful: exc_info formatting. logging.Formatter.format uses record.exc_info and appends traceback; our budget shouldn't include it. Since the truncated message ends before traceback appended, fine. Provide exc_text? If cached exc_text from a previous handler formatting... logging's format uses `self.formatException(record.exc_info)` if record.exc_text is None... Actually code:\n\n```python\nif record.exc_info:\n    if not record.exc_text:\n        record.exc_text = self.formatException(record.exc_info)\nif record.exc_text:\n    ...\n```\n\nOn the copy, exc_text copied from original dict — includes traceback text; unchanged from what would be produced. Fine.\n\nAlso stack_info: uses record.stack_info directly.\n\nNow `record.args = None` - getMessage: `if self.args: msg = msg % self.args`. So set args to None good. Or `()`.\n\nAlso set `record.message = truncated` maybe not required since super().format sets it.\n\nTruncate function:\n\n```python\n_OMISSION = \"...<omitted>...\"\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, max_chars: int) -> str:\n    if len(message) <= max_chars:\n        return message\n    marker = _OMISSION\n    remaining = max_chars - len(marker)\n    if remaining <= 0:\n        return message[:max_chars]  # shouldn't happen since max_chars>=32 > len(marker)=14\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return message[:head] + marker + (message[len(message)-tail:] if tail else \"\")\n```\n\nCheck budget 32: remaining = 32-15? marker \"...<omitted>...\" length: 3 dots + \"<omitted>\" 9 chars? \"<omitted>\" = 1+7+1 = 9, plus 3+3 = 15. remaining = 17, head = 9, tail = 8. Matches example (9 head, 8 tail, 15 marker). \n\nDefault init in _setup_logger: `max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`. Need import envs in logger.py. Careful about circular imports: does environ import logger? environ imports os, threading, datetime. No circular. But logger imports envs — check environ doesn't import logger. It doesn't (only shown top imports). Good.\n\nBut wait, `from deepinfer import environ` maybe use `from deepinfer.environ import envs`. Check whether other modules import pattern. stats_logger imports `from deepinfer.logger import init_logger`. Let's use `from deepinfer.environ import envs`.\n\nCareful: accessing envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS at import time `_setup_logger()` triggers parse and cache — that's intended (\"首次访问配置时读取环境变量\"). But if env var invalid, logger import fails with ValueError. Tests probably set var then import. Hmm, but potential issue: default module import `_setup_logger()` runs at import; if the env var is set to invalid value, import raises ValueError — that's expected behavior probably.\n\nHowever a concern: cached value at first access. If test does `envs.rearm(...)` then `_setup_logger()`, our init reads config value → new budget. Good.\n\n3. stats_logger: `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_