opy). But then formatTime uses record_copy.created — same. Exception info: record_copy.exc_info shared tuple — formatException doesn't mutate. stack_info same. Good.\n\nBut careful: the multi-line alignment check uses record.message — after super().format, record_copy.message is set = getMessage() = truncated, so use record_copy.message.\n\nAlso need record.keep_full_message exactly True: `if getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True: skip truncation`.\n\nNote: If exc_text present etc. Fine.\n\nAlso appended exception text is part of msg but not truncated (since we only truncate the message body). Good.\n\nImplementation in format:\n\n    def format(self, record):\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            message = record.getMessage()\n            if len(message) > self.max_message_chars:\n                budget = self.max_message_chars - len(_OMIT_MARKER)\n                head = (budget + 1) // 2\n                tail = budget - head\n                truncated = f\"{message[:head]}{_OMIT_MARKER}{message[len(message)-tail:] if tail else ''}\"\n                record = copy.copy(record)\n                record.msg = truncated\n                record.args = ()\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        ...\n\nWait budget when max=32: 32-15=17? Marker \"...<omitted>...\" length: 3 dots + 9 chars \"<omitted>\" = 9? \"<omitted>\" is 9 characters (< o m i t t e d > = 9). plus \"...\" \"...\" = 6 → 15. So budget 17, head = ceil(17/2)=9, tail=8. Matches example. Good.\n\nBeware tail slicing when tail==0: message[-0:] returns whole message — guard. With budget>=17, head>=9,tail>=8, fine but guard anyway.\n\nAlso `self.max_message_chars` must be set before super().__init__? no.\n\nValidation:\n\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n                raise ValueError(f\"max_message_chars must be an int >= 32 or None, got {max_message_chars!r}\")\n            if max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(...)\n\nBut default init reading config: _setup_logger creates NewLineFormatter(..., max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS). Careful: reading the config each init. If process env has invalid value, ValueError on init — acceptable.\n\nHmm, but reading config in _setup_logger at import time: logger module import triggers _setup_logger at module bottom presumably. Invalid env var would break import — tests likely check? The test says errors raised from config access. Risk: tests might set invalid env then import logger... probably test config only. Keep it.\n\nBut careful: existing tests may import logger with env unset → None → fine.\n\nstats_logger.py: `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nWait — logger.info with `%` in message? extra fine.\n\nNow let's check exact line in stats_logger and whether logger.info receives only m.\n\nLet me read the relevant snippets to confirm and get exact strings. Evidence shows:\n\n```python\n            for m in msgs:\n                logger.info(m)\n```\n\nNow also check `_setup_logger` and imports. Let me do a quick read of logger.py 60-110 to confirm exact text.\n\nActually evidence gives enough: _setup_logger body and NewLineFormatter. I'll read environ.py 1-30 for helpers to place the factory method cleanly.\n\nLet's act."}, "logprobs": null, "finish_reason": "stop"}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 10066, "completion_tokens": 1629, "total_tokens": 11695, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 4096}, "completion_tokens_details": {"reasoning_tokens": 1572}, "prompt_cache_hit_tokens": 4096, "prompt_cache_miss_tokens": 5970}, "system_fingerprint": "bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}, "classification": "ok"}
